 3. Google touts path to &quot;unhackable&quot; software 
 
 By Sam Sabin 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Google DeepMind researchers have used AI to mathematically prove that the encryption code protecting billions of private messages works as intended.

 Why it matters : The research breakthrough could eventually help defenders build better software. "You can finally get to this world of unhackable software," said Swarat Chaudhuri, a research scientist at Google DeepMind.

 Driving the news: Google DeepMind said today it used AI agents powered by its Gemini 4 Carbon model to mathematically verify that the code underpinning Signal's encryption is secure.

 The agents generated more than 3 million lines of mathematical proofs in the process, allowing researchers to verify the software at a level of detail previously considered impractical. To test the system, researchers reintroduced two previously fixed bugs into Signal's code. In both cases, the AI-powered framework generated mathematical proofs showing that the altered software was insecure. The intrigue: Chaudhuri told Axios that the AI lab's tools were able to accomplish this feat in just four weeks — compared with the years it would've likely taken humans alone.

